## What I did

The defect. generate_commit_message only recorded a path when it saw +++ b/. git renders a whole-file delete as --- a/<path> / +++ /dev/null — the path appears only on the --- line, which nothing read — so deleted files vanished from files_changed entirely and all four downstream decisions ran over a list missing them. Visible symptom: 26defce9 in my own history reads refactor(): remove code — empty parens, because join on an empty vec renders ().

The fix. Hold the --- a/ path in `pending_old_path; when the matching +++ turns out to be /dev/null, push *that* path as the hunk's file. The deletion's - lines follow the +++` line, so they land on the right index and the file carries real weight. An added file renders --- /dev/null, which doesn't match the --- a/ prefix, so the add path is untouched. No new git invocation; no change to the prefix vocabulary, the tie-break, the refactor/feat split, or any scope/summary string.

Verification. Five emission-point tests. The positive control was actually run: reverting only the parser hunk fails exactly tests 1, 3, 4, 5 and leaves both near-miss guards green. And the real thing — 26defce9's actual diff now yields refactor(mutants): remove code.

## Two places I corrected the task rather than following it

My expected prefix was wrong. I wrote chore(mutants); the code said refactor. mutants.toml is Code, and a pure deletion trips deletions > insertions * 2. The code was right — the prefix was always right on that commit; only the scope was destroyed. I fixed my assertion to the true value rather than the one I'd guessed.

The timeline was off, and the corrected version is worse. The task said "yesterday's session." It was today at 19:29 — commit 80c61784, +195 test lines, subject *"kill the 16 recorded survivors … and re-measure."* 26defce9 was authored at 20:03, 34 minutes later, with that perfect score already in hand.

I also checked the fixture claim instead of repeating it, and it's sharper than stated: dev/null occurs exactly once in the whole 623-line file at that commit — line 127, a comment inside the production branch that drops the path — and zero times in the test module (starting line 215). The branch acknowledged the shape in prose while not one fixture ever fed it.

I did not find a recorded "0 survivors" reading in dreams/experiments.jsonl (round 73 records 16 survivors, 32.0%). So CLAUDE.md cites what that commit's subject claims rather than asserting a number I couldn't verify. The durable point survives either way and is what I wrote down: a mutation score is bounded by what the fixtures can *ask. Every fixture entered that branch with `current = None` already set, so there was nothing to distinguish — "0 survivors" means no defect this population can phrase, never no defect*. That's my dream's own instrument catching its own detection threshold, one day after I started reading it.
